truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.12+deb1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Add t/rate-limit/08-concurrent-alloc.t. Drives N (default 10)
    concurrent qm-create-with-disk operations from independent forked
    PVE-side processes, then verifies all succeed, every child
    completed within a per-child wall-clock budget (default 120s), and
    total upstream auth.login_with_api_key count stays at <=1. The
    earlier concurrent test (07) only used cheap read RPCs; this one
    drives the full alloc path (pool.dataset.create +
    iscsi.extent.create + iscsi.targetextent.create + PVE-side iSCSI
    login/multipath/udev). Tunable via RL_CONCURRENT_N and
    RL_CONCURRENT_BUDGET env vars. No code changes.

 -- TrueNAS Proxmox Plugin Maintainers <noreply@github.com>  Tue, 23 Jun 2026 13:00:00 +0000

truenas-proxmox-plugin (2.1.11+deb1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * _broker_rpc now has a deadline-bounded round trip. Without this a
    wedged broker (stuck upstream WS, lost middlewared, hung systemd
    unit) blocked every PVE operation indefinitely because sysread on
    the Unix socket would never return. Per-call timeout is governed by
    tn_broker_timeout (seconds, default 30) and applies to the entire
    round trip, not per sysread, so a slow-but-progressing TN response
    won't trip it. IO::Select's can_write/can_read with remaining time
    is used between every syscall; on timeout we die with a clear
    "broker: read|write timeout after Ns" message that bubbles through
    the normal error path.
  * Add t/rate-limit/06-broker-restart.t and
    t/rate-limit/07-concurrent-broker.t to the regression suite to
    exercise (a) plugin recovery after the broker is restarted out
    from under it and (b) broker behaviour under concurrent
    in-process load (5 forked children x 4 calls each, asserting <=1
    upstream auth across the whole burst).

